 **User UI target** (decided design in `docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-13/user-tiles.md`, originating at commit 4a309f5ff): every HTML render an agent produces is a **surface**. The most recently updated surface is the **canvas** (primary); a pin overrides recency. A chat is a chat surface with a reply box, reusing `seon.render.web/message-bar-html` → inbound plus `:seon.message/caused-by` as reply-to. Data such as workout history has its own surface through its schema’s render. Reuse existing render dispatch and whole-view delivery; “tile” is retired. Only the reply box/routing needed by Intake enters these phases; canvas selection, pinning and other data surfaces remain targets outside this round.
 
+**Owner ruling — ordinary agents all the way down.** The user is an ordinary agent, identified by `:seon.agent/id` (for example `"sean"`), with no special user model. `/agent/{id}` is the HTML render of the viewed agent’s **entity**: its stored one-hop attachments—task, messages, evaluations in agent order, calls—each through its declared render. The prompt is the AI render of that same entity value. Selection belongs to the entity and its attachments; only the final face differs. No new context selector, page view model or rendering pipeline.
+
+Everything typed is an eval request **as the user’s agent**, through the same REPL entrance MCP `eval_clj` uses with `:agent`; it is recorded as the user’s evaluation. A plain reply submits `(my.message/send {:my.message/to viewed-agent :my.message/content text :my.message/caused-by question-id})` using the request keys below. This produces a message from the user; root’s `db<->flow` delivers it to the viewed agent’s branch (I1), with stored `:seon.message/caused-by` pointing to the question. The browser does not write messages through a separate entrance.
+
+On each view, the user’s context checks out a branch newly forked from the viewed agent’s current branch head. They see that program and data; their writes remain on their own branch. This forks the database branch, not the viewed agent’s private SCI context; ordinary agent context acquisition supplies the user’s environment. Existing branch lifecycle retains any earlier unmerged user work rather than overwriting it.
+
 ## Interfaces
 
 **Target contracts for the acceptance tests; register new names in their owning schema files before bodies.** Existing error unions remain at their entrances; examples use values acquired by the standard fixtures, not fabricated entities. New request/result schemas below are values, not lifecycle attributes. `main`, `candidate`, `base`, `connection`, `held`, `prepared`, `service` and message ids in examples are those held fixture values.
